  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1217 PM CDT Tue Aug 25 2026\n\nA warm front is moving through the region mid-day today, \nbringing a few scattered showers and isolated thunderstorms to \nthe tip of the Arrowhead and entering north-central Wisconsin. \nThese storms are part of what is Round 1 and are not expected \nto be severe. Behind the warm front, atmospheric recovery will \noccur this afternoon and last into the early evening hours \nwithin the short-lived warm sector of a low pressure system \ncentered over Manitoba, Minnesota, and Ontario. As daytime \nheating maximizes instability, isolated severe thunderstorms are\npossible beginning first in the far northern Minnesota \nBorderlands late this afternoon, with an onset time around 3 to\n4 PM.\n\nThe hazard threats of Large Hail and damaging wind gusts will \nbuild southeastward early this evening towards the Twin Ports. \nBy later this evening, closer to sunset, the severe threat moves\ninto into northwest Wisconsin and the eastern Arrowhead. Severe\nstorm chances are expected to taper shortly after sunset this \nevening with a cold frontal passage occurring tonight. Behind \nthe front, lingering cold air advection showers will continue \nthrough the overnight hours, but these will only bring very \nlocalized and light rainfall to the Northland.\n\nFor Wednesday, a slight cool down is forecast and breezier \npost- frontal regime takes hold across the area. Expect gusty \nnorthwest winds sustained around 10 to 15 mph with gusts \nreaching 20 to 30 mph. The exception for the cool down will be \nthe North Shore with those northwest winds downsloping and \naiding in warmer temperatures for the immediate coastal areas.\n\nThe light and localized rain showers from the overnight hours \nwill last into the Wednesday daytime period, actually increasing\nin coverage under daytime heating and instability. A few \ngeneral thunderstorms are forecast Wednesday afternoon within \nthis regime of numerous light and very localized rain showers. \nSevere weather is not anticipated on Wednesday.\n\nMinimal weather concerns are expected for Thursday and Friday as\npassing high pressure brings a return to quiet and dry conditions\nacross the region. It will feel fairly dry each afternoon with\nminimum relative humidity dropping to 35 to 45 percent\nrange...winds generally remain around or less than 15 mph though\nso no fire weather conditions are forecast.\n\nLooking ahead to the weekend, warm air advection sets up again Friday\nnight into Saturday morning as a weak passing front and a low to\nmid-level ridge axis slides eastward into the northern Plains. This\ncould be a time of a few scattered thunderstorms late Friday night\ninto Saturday morning. Furthermore, strong thunderstorms cannot be\nruled out later Saturday or at some point Sunday. A low pressure\nsystem in far northern Ontario and a reinforcing frontal passage\nforecast for Sunday will keep the pattern active, as convection over\nthe Northern Plains may spill over and slowly decay into the\nNorthland. Pending any increases in sky cover or lingering\nprecipitation, Sunday could actually be somewhat warm into the\nmid-80s for locations south of US Hwy 2. Generally minimal\nweather concerns to start next week under seasonable\ntemperatures and mainly dry conditions likely.",
